# Security

## XSS prevention
- Never use `v-html` with unsanitized user or third-party content. If `v-html` is unavoidable, sanitize with a library such as DOMPurify first, applied at the point of insertion.
- Avoid `eval()`, `new Function()`, and dynamically constructing DOM via `innerHTML` outside of Vue's templating.
- Be cautious binding user-controlled values into `href`, `src`, or `style` attributes — validate/allowlist protocols (block `javascript:` URLs).

## Environment variables & secrets
- Only variables prefixed `VITE_` are exposed to client code — never put API keys, secrets, or credentials meant to stay server-side in a `VITE_`-prefixed variable, since anything exposed this way ships in the client bundle and is publicly visible.
- Secrets (API keys for paid services, DB credentials, signing keys) belong in a backend/serverless function, never in frontend code or `.env` files that get bundled.
- Add `.env`, `.env.local`, and similar files to `.gitignore`; only commit `.env.example` with placeholder values.

## API & data handling
- Validate and sanitize all data received from APIs before rendering, even from "trusted" internal APIs.
- Use HTTPS for all API calls; never hardcode `http://` endpoints for production.
- Attach auth tokens via headers (e.g. `Authorization: Bearer`), not in URL query strings, to avoid leaking tokens in logs/browser history.
- Set reasonable timeouts and handle errors from `fetch`/`axios` calls explicitly; don't let unhandled rejections leak stack traces to the UI.
- Avoid storing sensitive tokens in `localStorage` when possible; prefer `httpOnly` cookies set by the backend. If `localStorage`/`sessionStorage` must be used, understand it's readable by any script on the page (XSS risk).

## Dependencies
- Prefer well-maintained, widely-used packages; check for recent security advisories before adding a new dependency for a small task.
- Recommend running `npm audit` (or the project's package manager equivalent) periodically and keeping dependencies patched.
- Pin dependency versions in `package.json` for reproducible, auditable builds where the project already does so.

## Routing & access control
- Enforce auth/route guards in `router.beforeEach`, but treat this as UX only — always re-check authorization server-side, since client-side route guards can be bypassed.
- Don't rely on hiding UI elements (`v-if="isAdmin"`) as a security boundary; the underlying API must independently enforce permissions.

## Headers & config
- Recommend a Content-Security-Policy (CSP) at the hosting/CDN level restricting script/style/img sources; avoid `unsafe-inline`/`unsafe-eval` where feasible.
- Recommend setting `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, `X-Frame-Options` (or `frame-ancestors` in CSP), and `Referrer-Policy` at the server/CDN layer.
- If using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`-equivalents or third-party embeds/iframes, use `sandbox` attributes and restrict permissions.
